{{also|hƿæt}}
==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ang|hƿæt}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*hwat}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*kʷod}}. Cognates include {{cog|ofs|hwet}} ({{cog|fy|wat}}), {{cog|osx|hwat}} ({{cog|nds|wat}}), {{cog|odt|wat}} ({{cog|nl|wat}}), {{cog|goh|hwaz}} ({{cog|de|was}}), {{cog|non|hvat}} ({{cog|sv|vad}}), {{cog|got|𐍈𐌰}}. The Indo-European root also led to {{cog|la|quod}}, {{cog|lt|kàd}}, and {{cog|ga|cad}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ʍæt/|lang=ang}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|ang|pronoun|g=n}}

# [[what]]
#* ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=trkLAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA188 The Dialogues of Solomon and Saturn]''
#*: ''Saga mē, '''hwæt''' sind þā þrēo þing þe nān mann būtan libban ne mæġ?''
#*:: Tell me, '''what''' are the three things that no one can live without?
#* '''c. 995''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://archive.org/stream/grammatik00aelfuoft#page/n129/mode/2up ''Extracts on Grammar in English'']
#*: ''Dǣdlīcu word ġeswitolaþ '''hwæt''' menn dōþ.''
#*:: Active verbs express '''what''' people do.
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=2xobVW92JLcC&pg=PA182 "Midlent Sunday"]
#*: ''Mid '''hwām''' mage wē byċġan hlāf þissum folce?''
#*:: '''What''' can we use to buy bread for these people?
#* ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=8UQrAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA116 The Legend of St. Andrew]''
#*: ''Ealle menn flēoþ of þǣre ċeastre. Tō '''hwon''' wille ġē þider faran?''
#*:: Everyone's fleeing that city. '''What''' do you want to go there for?
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=m2hVAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA182 "St. Benedict, Abbot"]
#*: ''Ān ġelēafful ierðling bær his dēadan suna līċ tō Benedictes mynstre, and mid drēorigum wōpe hrīemde tō þām hālgan were, "Āġief mē mīnne sunu, āġief mē mīnne sunu!" Se hālga wer andwyrde, "'''Hwæt''' lā, ætbræġd iċ þē þīnne sunu?" Se ierðling andwyrde, "Lā lēof, hē is dēad. Gang tō and ārǣr hine."''
#*:: A pious farmer carried his dead son's corpse to Benedict's monastery, and wept and screamed to the saint, "Give me back my son, give me back my son!"  The saint answered, "'''What''', did I take your son from you?" "Oh, father," replied the farmer, "he is dead. Go and raise him back to life."
# [[why]]
#* '''c. 900''', ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=At1SV29bH4oC&pg=PA38 The Consolation of Philosophy]''
#*: '''''Hwæt''' seofast þū wiþ mē?''
#*:: '''Why''' are you groaning at me?
# [[anything]], [[something]]
#* '''c. 900''', [https://books.google.com/books?id=xBNXAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA71 translation] of [[w:Orosius|Orosius']] ''History Against the Pagans''
#*: ''Sēo lēo bringþ hungrigum hwelpum '''hwæt''' tō etenne.''
#*:: The lion brings hungry cubs '''something''' to eat.

